.TH BASHIFY 1 "2026-07-30" "bashified" "Zach's utilities"
.SH NAME
bashify \- state a project's promise as a contract a shell can hold
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B bashify
[\fIlist\fR]
.br
.\" bashify: norun
.B bashify emit
\fIproject\fR \fIverb\fR \fIsummary\fR
.br
.\" bashify: norun
.B bashify page
\fIverb\fR \fIcommand\fR
.br
.\" bashify: norun
.B bashify check
\fIpage\fR \fIcommand\fR
.br
.\" bashify: norun
.B bashify amend
\fIpage\fR
.br
.B bashify
[\fB\-\-json\fR] [\fB\-q\fR|\fB\-\-quiet\fR] [\fB\-h\fR|\fB\-\-help\fR] [\fB\-\-version\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B bashify
is the verb of
.BR basheur .
To bashify a thing is to write down what it promises, in a form a shell can
check, so that the general intelligence standing in for it becomes
unnecessary.
.PP
The unit of work is a
.BR "man page" .
Not as documentation of a finished tool \- as the
.B contract
the tool is then obliged to satisfy. A page is written before its utility is
complete, and the utility is judged against the page rather than the reverse.
This is why
.B bashify
is the only utility in this family whose own subject matter is man pages: it
writes the promises the others are held to, and it is held to one itself.
.PP
Four acts, in the order they occur:
.TP
.B emit
Read a project's real repository, discover the tooling that exists, and
produce a
.B bashified
branch carrying a verb\-named utility, its man page, its contract, and a
contract test. The branch is built in a
.BR git\-worktree (1),
so a project's working tree is never touched, even mid\-session. Discovery
reads every tracked script anywhere in the tree, not only
.IR bin/ ,
because a project that keeps its tooling elsewhere otherwise ships a utility
silently missing most of itself.
.TP
.B page
Write or rewrite the man page for one verb, standalone, against a live
command.
.TP
.B check
Score a man page against the nine\-row page test and report the rows that
fail, the limits of the run, and the exit codes it could not provoke. Exits
.B 7
when the page fails a row: the page failing is not this utility failing, so
it is not reported as 5. See
.B THE PAGE TEST
below.
.TP
.B amend
Change a promise. This is a different act from keeping one, and it is gated;
see
.B FULFILLING VERSUS AMENDING
below.
.PP
Run
.B bashify list
to see which of these are backed by tooling here and now. A subcommand this
page names but which has no tooling behind it exits
.B 4
and says what is missing. It never exits 0 having done nothing.
.SH THE PAGE TEST
.B bashify check
scores nine rows. A page passes when a reader with no access to the
repository can predict the tool's behaviour from the page alone, and every
one of those predictions is machine\-checkable.
.TP
.B 1 NAME
One clause. An "and" in the NAME line means the utility is two utilities.
.TP
.B 2 SYNOPSIS
Every form shown runs as written.
.TP
.B 3 SURFACE
Bidirectional. Every flag and subcommand on the page exists; every flag and
subcommand that exists is on the page. An undocumented surface fails as hard
as a documented ghost.
.TP
.B 4 EXIT
Every code the page lists is reachable by some invocation, and every code the
tool can return is listed.
.TP
.B 5 EXAMPLES
Doctests. Each is executed and its stated output matches. An illustrative
example is a falsehood with a shell prompt in front of it.
.TP
.B 6 COST
Whether the tool can spend money is answerable from the page alone. Silence
fails.
.TP
.B 7 LINEAGE
SEE ALSO names the standard tools this one behaves like, so expectations can
be transferred instead of a dialect learned.
.TP
.B 8 PURGE
No vendor and no external paid service is named anywhere on the page, in its
examples, or in the paths it prints.
.TP
.B 9 TENSE
Present tense only. The page states what is. What is merely intended belongs
in
.I GAPS.md
and exits 4 at the call site.
.SH FULFILLING VERSUS AMENDING
Keeping a promise and changing a promise are different acts with different
gates, and conflating them is how a contract stops meaning anything.
.PP
.B Fulfilling
leaves the page byte\-identical and moves the implementation toward it. It
needs no permission and is what most work is. Success is rows going from fail
to pass with the page untouched.
.PP
.B Amending
changes the page, so it changes the promise.
.B bashify amend
gates it on four things: a stated reason naming what the tool learned that
the page did not know; the previous page preserved in version control rather
than overwritten in place; a full re\-run of the nine rows against the new
text; and a search for callers of the verb across bashified branches, since a
changed promise breaks a downstream pipeline silently.
.PP
The failure this gate exists to catch is amending a contract to match a
broken implementation. If the only thing that changed is that the tool cannot
do what it said, that is exit
.B 4
and a line in
.IR GAPS.md ,
not an edit to the page.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-json
Machine\-readable output. Honoured by
.BR list .
Requested anywhere else it exits 4 rather than being ignored, because a flag
that is silently dropped is a caller lied to.
.TP
.BR \-q ", " \-\-quiet
Results on standard output, commentary suppressed. Honoured everywhere.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Usage summary, including the exit vocabulary.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the utility name and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-
End of flags; everything after is a positional argument.
.SH THE COST BOUNDARY
.B bashify does not spend money and has no \-\-summon flag.
Every act it performs is a shell reading files. Passing
.B \-\-summon
is a usage error here, and so is any abbreviation of it.
.PP
A utility in this family that
.I can
spend declares
.B \-\-summon
and refuses to spend without it. There is deliberately no short form:
.B \-s
collides with existing tools and
.B \-S
differs from it by one shift key, which is an unacceptable property for the
only flag that costs real money. Spelling the word out is the deliberateness.
Because the flag is absent when the tool cannot spend,
.B \-\-help
alone answers "can this cost me anything?".
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B BASHIFY_WORK
Directory in which
.B emit
builds its worktrees. Defaults to a scratch path.
.TP
.B BASHIFY_REPO
The real repository, when a project lives inside a larger one it does not own
and its registered path holds no repository of its own.
.TP
.B BASHIFY_SCOPE
Limits discovery to one subtree, so a pass can never claim files belonging to
a co\-owner of a shared repository.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The promise was kept.
.TP
.B 2
Usage error; the caller is wrong.
.TP
.B 4
GAP: the tooling to keep this promise does not exist yet. The subcommand
names what is missing on standard error.
.TP
.B 5
BROKEN: it ran with valid arguments and failed to finish.
.TP
.B 6
BLIND: it cannot read what it was asked to judge \- a page that does not
exist, or a subject that is not executable. This is "I cannot see", never
"nothing to report", and it is never reported as a pass.
.TP
.B 7
The page under test failed at least one row. Specific to
.BR check ,
and declared above the shared vocabulary rather than reusing one of its
codes, because the subject failing is not the tool failing.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I bin/bashify
This utility.
.TP
.I bashify.sh
The implementation
.B emit
runs.
.TP
.I lib/check.sh
The page test
.B check
runs.
.TP
.I skel/lib/verb.sh
The shared runtime: one copy of the argument grammar, the cost boundary, and
the exit vocabulary, so utilities cannot drift into dialects.
.TP
.I skel/test/contract-test.sh
The contract test. It takes any command, so the same assertions run against a
legacy implementation and its replacement.
.TP
.I GAPS.md
What this page names and the tooling cannot yet do.
.SH EXAMPLES
List the subcommands and whether each is backed by tooling:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ bashify list
GAP           amend
MECHANIZED    check
MECHANIZED    emit
MECHANIZED    list
GAP           page
.fi
.RE
.PP
Score a page. The page and the command it describes are both named, so one
page can be scored against a legacy implementation and its replacement:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ bashify check man/bashify.1
bashify: check takes exactly 2 arguments: <page> <command> (got 1)
$ echo $?
2
.fi
.RE
.PP
Ask for a subcommand that is not built. The request is refused loudly, not
answered emptily:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ bashify amend man/bashify.1
bashify: GAP: amend: the four-step contract-change gate is not mechanised yet
bashify: no tooling exists for this yet; see GAPS.md
$ echo $?
4
.fi
.RE
.PP
Ask for machine\-readable output from a subcommand that does not produce it.
The flag is refused rather than dropped:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ bashify page dose bin/dose --json
bashify: GAP: --json: not wired for this subcommand
bashify: no tooling exists for this yet; see GAPS.md
$ echo $?
4
.fi
.RE
.SH HISTORY
Written 2026-07-30, before three of the four subcommands it describes
existed, which is the method rather than an oversight: the page is the
specification, and
.B list
reports which parts of it are specification only.
.PP
Amended the same day, when
.B check
was built at its own exit\-4 call site and scored this page 6 of 9. The
amendment records what the tooling learned that the page did not know: that a
scorer needs an exit code meaning "the subject failed", that a form carrying
placeholder arguments has to declare itself unrunnable, and that two of the
examples had gone false the moment
.B check
started working. The previous text is in version control.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR man (1),
.BR git-worktree (1),
.BR make (1),
.BR test (1),
.BR getopt (1)
.PP
Lineage: the exit vocabulary follows
.BR test (1)
in reserving 0 for success and giving distinct nonzero codes distinct
meanings; the flag grammar follows
.BR getopt (1)
conventions including
.B \-\-
as end\-of\-flags; the specification\-before\-implementation posture is
.BR make (1)-like
in that the page states a target and the tooling reports what it cannot yet
build.
